@startuml client_connection_endpoint_callbacks_activity
title ClientConnection - registered endpoint callbacks

start
if (Callback is input?) then (yes)
    :reason = ProcessInputEvent();
    if (reason != kNone?) then (yes)
        if (TrySetStopReason(reason) succeeds?) then (yes)
            :ProcessStateChange(kStopping);
            :UnregisterPosixEndpoint(posix_endpoint_);
            note right
                Disconnect dispatch invokes SwitchToStopState().
                It is a separate registered callback, not an extra
                direct call made by this input wrapper.
            end note
        else (no)
            :Preserve the existing stop reason and cleanup owner;
        endif
    else (no)
        :No stop transition requested by this input result;
    endif
else (no)
    :The callback is not input;
    if (Callback is ping?) then (yes)
        if (notify_callback_ is non-empty?) then (yes)
            :Invoke notify_callback_ with an empty span;
        else (no)
            :Ignore this ping;
        endif
    else (disconnect)
        :SwitchToStopState();
        note right
            This lambda does not select another reason or repeat the gate.
            Shared finalization asserts kStopping, a selected stop reason,
            and engine callback context.
        end note
    endif
endif

:Return from the invoked callback;
stop

legend
    These callbacks are installed on the embedded endpoint by TryConnect().
    The output callback is empty; there is no client output-event handler here.
    API Stop() and input failure compete for the same stop transition.
    Only the input winner publishes kStopping and unregisters the endpoint.
    A losing input path leaves the API winner's cleanup in place.
    Ping delivery is distinct from protocol NOTIFY and does not receive a message.
    Disconnect dispatch timing is an engine boundary, not fixed by this view.
endlegend

@enduml